          { name: "Renter and Young Professional Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 30.1% renter rate in a district this affluent signals a meaningful constituency of young professionals and working adults who are not yet homeowners — and who represent a significant share of the 41% Democratic opposition. Housing affordability, student debt policy, and whether local government removes barriers to economic entry are the issues that activate this constituency and make them persuadable. A candidate who speaks to these concerns with specificity rather than dismissing them as outside the representative's scope builds a cross-class coalition that holds the seat against opposition challenge." },
          { name: "Demographic Change and the 4.4% Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The ~4.4% gap between reported Census categories and 100% in a suburban Wisconsin district likely includes mixed-race households, recent immigrant communities, and younger adults in demographic transition — constituencies whose economic and social priorities may differ from both the white homeowner majority and the reported minority populations. In districts at this income and education level, demographic change often correlates with shifting issue priorities on education, taxation, and community character. Ignoring this gap means potentially missing the constituency whose growth and mobilization patterns will determine competitiveness in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "At 43.1% college education and $90,904 income, WI-HD-15 is a constituency of educated professionals who are skeptical of partisan extremism and responsive to governance competence arguments rather than partisan loyalty appeals. A candidate who campaigns on partisan messaging, culture-war positioning, or attacks on the opposition's character rather than on fiscal results and school quality is misreading the electorate and conceding persuadable voters to an opponent who speaks their language. The 41% opposition includes many college-educated professionals and suburban professionals who are authentically persuadable on governance grounds." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result in a district with 43% college education, 30% renters, and $90k+ income is not a safe hold — it is a working majority held in a constituency that evaluates on governance competence and policy delivery. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 59% represents a comfortable floor and runs a low-engagement campaign will find it genuinely competitive against an opponent who takes school funding, property taxes, or housing affordability seriously. This is a high-engagement, issues-driven electorate that demands detailed governance arguments, not inherited partisan margins." }
