          "Engage the 30% renter constituency and college-educated professionals directly: renters and younger professionals in this income and education bracket evaluate on housing affordability, student debt policy, and whether local government removes barriers to economic mobility. The 41% Democratic share suggests these constituencies are organized and persuadable — a candidate who ignores them concedes 30-40% of the district to an opposition narrative about whose side government is on.",
          "Understand the 4.4% demographic gap and suburban demographic change: in Wisconsin suburban and exurban districts, the gap between reported Census categories and 100% often includes mixed-race households, immigrant communities, and younger adults in economic transition. These constituencies may have different priorities than either the white homeowner majority or the reported Asian and Hispanic populations suggest — and their growth rate relative to the overall population is a baseline competitiveness signal.",
          "Do not campaign this seat as a safe Republican hold: 59-41 in a district with 43% college education, substantial renter representation, and meaningful Democratic organizational infrastructure is a working margin, not a durable one. Any incumbent who consolidates only the existing Republican base and relies on the 59% floor while ignoring the 41% opposition's governance arguments will find this seat genuinely competitive in the next challenging cycle."
