          { name: "Wage & Economic Mobility for Working Families", tag: "lean-into", why: "With median household income at $50,115 and 33.5-year median age, the constituency is in its peak earning years but has not yet accumulated substantial household wealth; wage stagnation, healthcare costs, and childcare expenses are the binding constraints on upward mobility. A representative who can articulate and advance concrete wage policy — minimum wage indexing, sector-specific wage standards, enforcement of prevailing wage in public contracting — and address childcare affordability will build genuine constituent loyalty beyond the baseline 82% partisan margin." },
          { name: "Cross-Racial Working-Family Coalition Building", tag: "careful", why: "The district is 50.47% Black, 34.37% white, and 7.63% Hispanic, with shared economic pressures across racial lines (61.7% renters, $50,115 income) that could support a unified political narrative around working-family economic security. However, framing these issues through a racial rather than economic lens — or serving Black and white constituencies through separate political channels — risks fragmenting the coalition. A representative who leads with the material economic concerns that unite renters and working families across racial lines will have more durable support than one who depends on racial bloc mobilization without delivering on shared economic interests." },
          { name: "Treating 82% as a Durable Safe Seat Without Active Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "An 82% result in a majority-Black urban district reflects baseline Democratic performance, not an affirmative vote of confidence in the incumbent's performance on renter affordability, wage policy, or economic mobility. The incumbent has secure partisan protection, but that protection extends only as far as active constituent engagement on the material issues that define daily life for 61.7% renters at $50,115 income. A representative who rests on the 82% without delivering specific legislative wins on housing and wages will lose credibility and create an opening for a primary challenger who can make a more compelling case." },
          { name: "Ignoring the College Attainment Gap (31.8%) as an Educational Policy Signal", tag: "avoid", why: "Wisconsin's statewide college attainment rate exceeds 35%; HD-18's 31.8% suggests either cost barriers preventing higher education access, workforce pathway misalignment between education and employment, or community college completion patterns that are not captured in four-year degree metrics. A representative who does not investigate and address the specific barriers to education completion — and who does not develop policy reflecting actual education-to-employment pathways in the district — is missing a material quality-of-life concern affecting younger adults' long-term earning potential and household stability." }
