          "Engage the 34.37% white constituency and the cross-racial economic coalition: at $50,115 income with 61.7% renters, housing and wage concerns cut across racial lines; a representative who frames these as shared working-family issues rather than racially segmented constituencies will build broader durability than one who depends on racial bloc voting.",
          "Address the college attainment gap (31.8%) relative to the younger median age (33.5): younger adults in this district are not pursuing or completing higher education at the rate of their peers elsewhere; investigate whether this reflects cost barriers, workforce pipeline misalignment, or community college vs. four-year degree patterns, and develop education-to-employment policy that reflects actual pathway data.",
          "Do not assume 82% is a stable floor: the margin is large in partisan terms, but it reflects a minimum Democratic baseline in a majority-Black urban district, not an endorsement of the incumbent's performance on the economic issues that matter most to renters and younger workers; active constituent engagement on housing, wages, and economic mobility is required to maintain this seat against any credible challenger who can articulate a better plan."
