          { name: "Framing District Concerns as 'Special Interests' Rather Than Core Constituent Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 35.3% renters and 14.94% Hispanic population, housing affordability, tenant protections, and immigrant family stability are not special-interest concerns — they are the material economic issues affecting a plurality of actual constituents. Any candidate whose framing positions renter advocacy, Hispanic economic mobility, or public education funding as special interests competing with 'regular' constituent priorities will find that framing actively disconnected from the lived economic reality of the district's demographic majority. The 61% reflects a constituency that evaluates on whether government is delivering concrete answers to their own economic security — not whether certain concerns are deemed worthy of legislative attention." }
