        issues: [
          { name: "Middle-Income Household Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $67,555 median income, 67% homeownership, and 40.7 median age, WI HD-26 is a working-family constituency for whom property tax fairness, school funding adequacy, and healthcare cost containment are direct material concerns. Sheehan's 51% margin suggests the prior Republican incumbent either failed to address these concerns with specificity or lost persuadable voters who evaluated on competence. A representative who leads with property tax policy, school funding, and household cost-of-living issues addresses the primary governance question this constituency asks: is government managing my household economics effectively?" },
          { name: "Growing Latino and Asian Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The combined 15.3% of the district represented by Latino (8.78%) and Asian (6.52%) communities signals a changing suburban electorate where these populations are likely growing from that baseline. In a 51-49 district, whether these communities perceive the incumbent as genuinely accountable to their economic and civic concerns — immigration policy, workforce opportunity, education access — determines whether they turn out and whether they shift margins. Active engagement with these communities as core constituencies, not afterthoughts, builds durability in a genuinely competitive seat." },
          { name: "Understanding the 49% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a district with 78.76% white, 67% homeowner composition, and solidly middle-class economics, the 49% Democratic opposition cannot be explained by demographics alone — it reflects either persuasion of the prior Republican incumbent or real policy dissatisfaction among voters who could plausibly vote Republican. Identifying whether that opposition is concentrated among college-educated suburban women concerned with social policy, working-class homeowners frustrated with taxes, or both is the prerequisite for understanding what must be maintained or changed to hold the seat." },
          { name: "Treating 51% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51% win in a district with near-parity demographics and middle-class economics is a competitive baseline, not a comfortable hold. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 51% represents a stable coalition while neglecting the 49% opposition will find the seat vulnerable in the next cycle when a challenger with specific policy remedies and authentic constituency engagement emerges." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The 33.1% renter rate in a homeowner-dominant district represents younger households, service-sector workers, and others for whom housing affordability and rental market stability are direct concerns. In a 51-49 seat, renter turnout and voter preference are mathematically sufficient to determine the outcome; treating renters as a minor constituency or assuming they will remain politically passive risks losing the margin to a challenger who offers concrete housing policy solutions." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Wisconsin HD-26: A Toss-Up Suburban Seat at 51-49, Where Middle-Income Homeowners and a Growing Latino Population Determine Control",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "WI HD-26 is a genuinely competitive suburban district that defies easy partisan categorization. Sheehan's 51% Democratic win in a district with 78.76% white, 67% homeowner composition, and $67,555 median household income signals a constituency that does not vote on inherited party affiliation — it votes on whether government is delivering for its material circumstances. The district is neither a wealthy suburb nor a struggling exurban zone; it is a solidly middle-class community where property taxes, school funding adequacy, cost-of-living pressures, and local service delivery are the practical governance concerns that swing votes. At 51-49, this seat represents a near-perfect partisan tie, and the 3-point Democratic margin reflects either soft Republican persuasion or specific dissatisfaction with the prior Republican incumbent — not a realignment toward Democratic identification. Any candidate holding this seat must understand that the 49% opposition is not a fringe; it is nearly half the constituency, and the margin is fragile enough that neglecting either the Democratic base or the persuadable Republican leaning voters will cost the seat in the next competitive cycle.",
          "The district's demographic composition — 78.76% white, 8.78% Hispanic/Latino, 6.52% Asian, 2.64% Black, with a ~3% gap likely reflecting mixed-race or other identification — reveals a changing suburban electorate where the Latino community's growth trajectory and the Asian community's presence together represent roughly 15% of the population and are likely growing. At a 24.3% college attainment rate and $67,555 median income, WI HD-26 is a working-professional and skilled-trades constituency where education and income are sufficient to demand policy competence but not so high as to produce an elite insulation from economic anxiety. The 33.1% renter rate — a meaningful minority in a homeowner-dominant district — signals younger households, service-sector workers, and others for whom housing affordability and rental market stability are direct quality-of-life concerns. The 40.7-year median age places this district squarely in the working-family cohort: school-age children, mortgage obligations, aging parents, and the full complexity of middle-class household economics are the lived experience of the constituency. A representative who can address property tax fairness, school funding, healthcare cost containment, and housing affordability with specific policy knowledge commands credibility in this district; one who defaults to abstract positioning or partisan messaging will find the seat vulnerable to challenge."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat 51-49 as a competitive baseline, not a durable majority: the 49% opposition in a middle-income suburban district suggests the prior Republican incumbent either lost persuadable voters on specific issues or faced a well-executed challenger campaign that shifted soft Republicans. Identify whether the opposition is concentrated among college-educated suburbanites concerned with social issues, working-class homeowners frustrated with property taxes, or both — and address those concerns directly rather than consolidating the existing Democratic base.",
          "Lead with middle-income household economic security: property tax fairness and school funding adequacy are the practical governance issues for a 40.7-year-old, 67% homeowner, $67,555-income constituency. This is not a wealthy suburb where education funding can be dismissed; it is a district where schools are funded by local property taxes and families are evaluating whether their taxes are buying adequate service.",
          "Engage the growing Latino and Asian communities with specific constituent attention and candidate presence: at 8.78% and 6.52% respectively, these communities together represent 15% of the district and are likely growing. Immigration policy, workforce opportunity, and whether these communities perceive the incumbent as genuinely accountable to their concerns are the factors determining whether they turn out and how they vote.",
          "Do not take the 33.1% renter constituency for granted: in a homeowner-dominant district, renters can be politically invisible despite representing one-third of the population. Housing affordability, tenant protections, and whether government is addressing the economic concerns of younger households and service-sector workers are the issues that determine whether renters mobilize — and in a 51-49 seat, renter turnout and persuasion are mathematically sufficient to determine the outcome."
        ]
