          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Participation & Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 11.48%, the Hispanic community is the second-largest demographic group in HD-31 and is growing faster than the overall population in Wisconsin—yet is not explicitly engaged in most Republican messaging in this district. Education quality, workforce development, business opportunity, and inclusive community institutions are the issues that determine whether Hispanic households integrate into the Republican coalition or drift toward Democratic alternatives. A candidate who addresses Hispanic constituents' economic concerns with specificity, rather than treating them as a monolithic voting bloc, builds the demographic durability that holds the seat across electoral cycles." },
          { name: "Renter & Younger-Adult Constituency Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "At 32% renters and 39.6 median age, nearly one-third of HD-31's households do not own property and are evaluating candidates on housing affordability, education costs, and economic mobility rather than property tax policy. This constituency is not inherently Democratic but is currently voting 37% opposition—suggesting that either their concerns are not being heard or they believe Democrats better address them. The specific composition of the opposition is crucial: if renters and younger adults are the swing factor, a 26-point margin can narrow significantly in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Dismissing or Ignoring Non-Homeowner Constituencies", tag: "avoid", why: "Any candidate who campaigns to the homeowner majority while implicitly or explicitly dismissing renters, younger adults, or Hispanic neighbors as outside the constituency is strategically surrendering the opportunity to expand the 63% result. In a state where statewide margins are regularly three to five percentage points, losing even five percentage points of renter support or Hispanic participation—a shift of 2-3 points of the overall vote—materially changes the competitive dynamics. Homeowner consolidation alone is not sufficient to hold the seat against a credible challenger who addresses these constituencies' material concerns." },
          { name: "Treating the 63% Margin as a Stable Base Rather Than a Coalition to Be Maintained", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 result in Wisconsin—a state where gubernatorial and Senate races are regularly decided by fewer than five percentage points—is not a commanding majority; it is a solid coalition built on the alignment of homeowner interests with Republican positioning on property tax and education spending, combined with Democratic weakness among rural and exurban constituencies. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 63% represents a durable floor rather than a coalition requiring active maintenance risks finding a credible challenger mobilizing the 37% opposition and peeling off swing homeowners on specific economic concerns. This seat demands sustained constituent engagement, not the confidence of a safe margin." }
