          { name: "Treating the 36% Opposition as Negligible or Inevitable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 result in a district with 29.3% college education, $68,519 income, and a 53.7-year median age is not an impregnable Republican fortress — it is a solid but not overwhelming margin held in a constituency that expects substantive governance. The 36% opposition likely includes college-educated professionals evaluating on policy specificity, older fixed-income households concerned about healthcare costs, and renters (16.5%) whose economic interests diverge from the homeowner majority. Any incumbent who assumes the 64% margin is durable without actively maintaining relationships across the opposition coalition risks losing ground in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Demographic Invisibility of the 7.7% Gap Population", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories (White 90.3%, Hispanic 1.95%, Black 0.41%, Asian 0.41%) sum to 93.1%, leaving a ~6.9% gap that is not accounted for in the district data. While smaller than other districts, this gap likely represents Native American, multiracial, or other minority residents whose needs around economic mobility, healthcare access, and community inclusion can become invisible when the dominant narrative centers on the 90.3% white homeowner majority. Failing to engage this minority constituency with specific attention risks both governance failures and latent electoral vulnerability." }
