          { name: "Property Tax & Rural Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Wisconsin districts with older homeowner majorities typically face structural pressures on local tax bases as younger populations leave and service costs rise — creating the exact squeeze that generates political vulnerability. Specific legislative solutions on property tax relief, rural school funding, and the cost of maintaining infrastructure for dispersed populations are the tangible governance issues that demonstrate a candidate understands what older homeowners actually need. A record of action on these issues is far more credible than abstract messaging about Republican values or fiscal conservatism." },
          { name: "Understanding the 32% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "In a district with 92.99% white population and strong homeownership, a 32% Democratic result is not explained by demographic diversity or renter mobilization alone — it reflects real persuadable voters or organized opposition whose concerns the incumbent has not addressed. Whether the 32% is concentrated among older homeowners frustrated by healthcare costs, working-class residents facing wage stagnation, or lower-propensity voters being mobilized by specific local failures, identifying and directly addressing what is generating that opposition is the difference between a 68% win that is genuinely durable and one that only appears safe because turnout favors the incumbent." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as a Stable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with below-average income, below-average education, and 32% active opposition, the 68% result is not a floor — it is a margin that depends on ongoing consolidation of older homeowner support and relative Democratic turnout. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 68% represents a comfortable hold, rather than as a number that requires active defense of older homeowners' economic interests, is misreading the district's underlying fragility. The next cycle could easily produce different turnout dynamics or a challenger who speaks more directly to economic security concerns." },
          { name: "Dismissing the Renter and Working-Class Minority", tag: "avoid", why: "With 19.3% renters and a working-class constituency embedded in the homeowner majority, ignoring housing affordability, wage, and economic mobility issues creates an opening for a challenger who frames these as missing from the incumbent's agenda. Even though homeowners dominate numerically, a candidate who is perceived as indifferent to the economic security of younger and working-class residents is vulnerable to mobilization around cost-of-living and opportunity issues. Acknowledgment of and specific attention to these concerns, even if they do not dominate the district's median voter, demonstrates responsive representation rather than single-constituency politics." }
