          "Consolidate the 56% rather than pursuing marginal growth: the demographic profile suggests limited persuasion upside from college-educated professionals (only 25.8%) and limited growth opportunity from demographic change; the incumbent's path to 60%+ requires activating and retaining the working and middle-class voters who delivered 56%, not pursuing new constituencies.",
          "Prepare for effective Republican opposition on economic management: the 44% opposition at this income level is not automatically driven by social conservatism — it likely reflects genuine disagreement on whether Democratic policies are managing economic security effectively; a strong challenger will make the cost-of-living case and require the incumbent to respond with specific, measurable benefits to the 56%."
