          { name: "Treating the District as Ideologically Driven Rather Than Economically Driven", tag: "avoid", why: "At 20.9% college attainment and $69,910 median income, this is a working-class district where constituent concerns are rooted in material economic security—housing affordability, wage stability, service quality, and economic mobility—rather than ideological positioning or partisan identity. A representative who pursues abstract conservative or liberal positioning without connecting it to these material concerns risks appearing disconnected from the actual lives of people in the district. The unopposed cycle is an opportunity to build a legislative record grounded in constituent economic concerns, not to establish a partisan messaging operation that talks past the working families who actually need government action." }
