          "The district's demographic composition — 69.62% white, 12.99% Hispanic/Latino, 6.34% Asian, 5.7% Black, and ~5.35% unaccounted for (likely Native American or multiracial) — does not suggest a monolithic Democratic base. This is a pluralistic, educated, working-to-professional-class district where the college-educated share (52.9%) is well above state average, but where the presence of substantial renter population and median age under 36 suggests younger families and professionals still building economic security. A Republican challenger who can credibly position themselves as addressing housing affordability, property tax fairness, and economic opportunity for young professionals — rather than defaulting to cultural conservative messaging — could plausibly compete for the college-educated, renter-heavy swing constituency. The 100% Democratic result masks an absence of organized Republican opposition, not an absence of competitive terrain."
