          { name: "Education Funding & K-12 Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 36.8% college-educated adults, HD-55 is a constituency of professionals and educated homeowners with direct stakes in public school quality, teacher compensation, and education funding adequacy. In a district where median income is $80,874 and property taxes are a meaningful household expense, voters evaluate whether state government is investing in schools at a level that protects long-term property values and community quality of life. The incumbent's record on education funding and any challenger's alternative agenda are the most direct tests of whether each candidate is aligning with the material priorities of the district's educated professional base." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost Management for Middle-Income Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 39.9-year median age in an $80,874 income district signals a constituency of working professionals and established families for whom healthcare costs — premiums, out-of-pocket maximums, prescription drug pricing, and the viability of rural hospitals serving suburban satellite communities — are direct household budget concerns. In a district with meaningful college education and professional employment, healthcare policy is not abstract; voters have health insurance, use it regularly, and evaluate whether state policy is keeping costs manageable relative to income. This is where educated swing voters are most likely to perceive the incumbent as either aligned with their interests or missing their priorities." },
          { name: "Property Tax Fairness & Government Efficiency", tag: "careful", why: "At 32.6% renter rate, the district has a constituency not directly affected by property tax burden; the 67.4% homeowner majority, however, are asset-holders acutely aware of property tax trends relative to home values and income. In an upper-middle-income suburb, property tax fairness — whether increases are justified by service delivery, whether assessment practices are transparent, whether spending is efficient — is a credibility issue for candidates. Incumbent and challenger alike must demonstrate either that current tax levels reflect good-value public services or that reform is underway; neglecting property tax concerns among affluent homeowners creates an opening for opposition messaging about government inefficiency." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture War Positioning as Primary Campaign Focus", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, upper-middle-income suburban voters in their 40s earning $80k+ are less responsive to abstract partisan culture war messaging than to concrete policy outcomes on education, healthcare, and tax burden. A candidate who leads on abortion, gender issues, or other polarizing cultural topics at the expense of engagement on education funding and healthcare access is signaling that the campaign is not about serving the district's actual household concerns — and that signal is particularly costly among college-educated swing voters who evaluate on policy substance rather than partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Taking the 60-40 Margin for Granted as a Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 36.8% of adults are college-educated and $80,874 median income places residents in the persuadable middle of the income distribution, a 60-40 result is not a commanding mandate — it is a plurality dependent on the incumbent's alignment with swing-voter priorities on education, healthcare, and government efficiency. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 60% represents a safe hold without actively engaging the college-educated professional swing voters whose defection would narrow the margin to competitive range is overestimating electoral security. The opposition does not need to win HD-55 decisively; it needs merely to persuade 5-7% of the college-educated professional base that the incumbent has underperformed on their priorities." }
