          { name: "Relying on Unopposed Status as Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% result is not a signal of permanent partisan dominance — it is a signal that no challenger emerged in the last cycle, which tells us nothing about whether one will emerge in the next. Unopposed incumbents often experience unexpected competitive challenges when a specific local grievance mobilizes opposition or when a credible challenger identifies an opening around tax burden, school performance, or development disputes. An incumbent who treats 100% as a baseline to manage downward rather than as a floor to build from will be vulnerable when conditions shift." },
          { name: "Ignoring Long-Term Suburban Electoral Volatility", tag: "avoid", why: "Wisconsin's affluent suburbs have demonstrated real electoral volatility in recent cycles as college-educated professionals have reweighted issues around institutional competence, local governance, and civic quality over straight partisan alignment. A district at 30.1% college attainment and high income that shows no signs of competitive challenge is a district where the incumbent may be misreading baseline stability as permanent electoral advantage. Complacency in candidate recruitment, messaging, or constituent engagement on local issues is the most common pathway from unopposed wins to sudden competitive vulnerability." }
