          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost for Older Homeowners", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 45.8 median age with 74.8% homeownership and $66,301 income, HD-73 is a constituency of older, asset-holding but income-constrained households for whom healthcare costs are rising faster than incomes. Medicare deductibles, prescription drug affordability, and the viability of rural and suburban healthcare infrastructure are direct economic security concerns. Stroud's 58% result reflects Democratic consolidation around these issues; any challenger must either recapture older homeowners on healthcare through credible Republican repositioning, or accept losing this critical demographic segment." },
          { name: "Social Security and Medicare Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 45.8-year median age and 74.8% homeownership at $66,301 income, this is a community where a plurality are within 10-15 years of or already on fixed-income retirement benefits. Protection of Social Security and Medicare against cuts, means-testing, or privatization is the single issue most likely to structure homeowner voting behavior. Republican candidates historically underperform with this demographic when the opposing party can credibly claim to be defending these programs; in a state where Democrats have owned this positioning, it is the primary arena where a challenger can shift the margin." },
          { name: "Property Tax Burden and Local Fiscal Stability", tag: "careful", why: "The 74.8% homeownership rate means property tax is a direct household expense for three-quarters of the district; at $66,301 income, property tax burden is a meaningful share of household budget for many families. Stroud's Democratic messaging may or may not have emphasized property tax relief; if homeowners perceive rising effective tax rates despite stable income, this becomes an opening for a challenger to reposition as the fiscal responsibility candidate. However, property tax is a complex issue where anti-tax rhetoric can backfire if constituents believe it threatens education funding or local services they depend on — require careful local economic analysis before leading on this issue." },
          { name: "Treating HD-73 as a Safe Democratic Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58-42 result in Wisconsin's current political geography is solid but not insurmountable; districts with this demographic profile (older, homeowner-heavy, non-college-educated majority) have shifted significantly toward Republicans in recent cycles when healthcare messaging is credible. Any Democratic incumbent who assumes this margin is durable without sustained engagement with older homeowners on economic security is vulnerable to a well-executed Republican challenge focused on Medicare/Social Security and fixed-income household economics." },
          { name: "Cultural or Identity-Based Messaging in a Working-Class District", tag: "avoid", why: "With 25.7% college attainment and 90.14% white homeowners at $66,301 income, HD-73 is not a district where abstract cultural or identity-based progressive messaging performs well — it is a constituency that evaluates on whether government delivers concrete benefits and protection for their household economics. Any candidate whose primary positioning emphasizes cultural issues rather than healthcare access, property tax fairness, and Social Security protection is misaligned with the district's actual voting drivers and is ceding the terrain where elections in this seat are won and lost." }
