          { name: "Working-Class and Small-Business Economic Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 22.6% college attainment — below state average — and $63,929 median income, WI-HD-75's economy is rooted in construction, agriculture, small manufacturing, and service sectors rather than knowledge work; these constituencies evaluate candidates on whether government policy supports or undermines their ability to earn a living and sustain a business. Legislative attention to workforce development, agricultural policy, construction industry regulation, and small-business tax fairness demonstrates alignment with how this district's economy actually functions. The absence of a large college-educated professional class means this constituency responds more reliably to practical economic stewardship than to ideological positioning or abstract conservative messaging." },
          { name: "Understanding the 33% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "A 67-33 Republican margin in a district this demographically uniform is solid but not dominant, and understanding whether the 33% opposition reflects specific economic frustration, local governance failures, or genuine ideological realignment is strategically important for maintaining and potentially expanding the current margin. In an aging, fixed-income-constrained constituency at $63,929 income, economic messaging that resonates across partisan lines — particularly around healthcare costs and property tax fairness — is capable of moving swing voters, but only if the representative takes the opposition's concerns seriously rather than dismissing it as negligible. The 33% represents real constituent economic anxiety that a credible challenger could mobilize if the current representative assumes the margin is stable." },
          { name: "Relying on Inherited Partisan Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where 78.4% are homeowners, median age is 46.4, and college attainment is 22.6% is not a credentials-based, ideologically mobile constituency — but it is a working-class and small-business constituency that votes based on whether government is delivering for their material economic interests, not on inherited partisan identity. Assuming that Republican voters in WI-HD-75 will vote Republican regardless of whether legislative policy serves their economic concerns is strategically vulnerable; a candidate who speaks directly to property tax burden, healthcare costs, and small-business viability will outperform a candidate who relies on ideological messaging and assumes the base will follow." },
          { name: "Treating 67% as a Safe Majority Requiring No Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67-33 margin is comfortable but not insurmountable, and a representative who uses that margin as license to govern primarily for ideological satisfaction rather than constituent economic service is vulnerable in a cycle where national economic conditions shift or where a local disruption (manufacturing closure, healthcare facility consolidation) concentrates voter attention on whether government delivered results. The 46.4 median age and $63,929 income profile indicates a constituency acutely aware of economic constraint; consistent legislative attention to local problem-solving and tangible policy results on property tax fairness, healthcare, and infrastructure maintenance is what makes the 67% durable across multiple cycles." }
