          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Community Engagement and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.23% Hispanic/Latino population in a 53% Democratic district is not a secondary constituency — it is a core component of Wagner's coalition whose turnout and candidate preference are swing factors. This community is younger on average, has strong upward-mobility expectations, and votes based on whether government policy and rhetoric treat it as an asset or a threat. Consistent engagement on wage policy, healthcare access, fair treatment, and the practical barriers to economic advancement is not optional outreach but a structural requirement for holding the seat. Neglect of this community or failure to deliver on material economic promises directly transfers votes to Republicans." },
          { name: "Turnout Mechanics and Demographic Consolidation", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this demographically favorable to Democrats — young, renters, working-class, 22% Hispanic — a 53% result suggests that either turnout among younger and Hispanic voters is being suppressed relative to potential, or that Republican economic messaging is successfully eroding Democratic support. Diagnosing which dynamic is operative is critical: if the issue is turnout, the representative must build reliable mobilization infrastructure targeting young voters and Hispanic households; if the issue is messaging erosion, the representative must sharpen economic argument and demonstrate concrete delivery on working-family concerns. Without clarity on this distinction, any strategic response will be incomplete." },
          { name: "Treating a 53% Margin as a Secure Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53% result in a district with the third-youngest median age in the state, nearly half renters, and median income below the state average is not a secure majority — it is a contested coalition held together by alignment between the representative's economic policy and voters' material circumstances. Young renters and working-family households do not have deep partisan roots; they vote based on whether their condition is improving or deteriorating relative to their expectations. Any representative who campaigns this seat as though 53% is a stable floor, rather than a coalition that requires constant active delivery and engagement, will find it flipped in a competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Identity Rather Than Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "WI HD-90 is not a district where inherited Democratic loyalty is the dominant factor — it is a district where voters evaluate based on whether government is delivering on economic mobility, housing affordability, and wage growth. A representative who assumes that young renters and working families will vote Democratic regardless of results, or who emphasizes partisan rhetoric over concrete economic policy, will lose credibility quickly with a constituency that is economically precarious and therefore highly attentive to whether politicians are actually improving their circumstances. Economic delivery and specificity are not luxuries in this district — they are mandatory." }
