          "The 48% Republican share is not a disorganized opposition — it represents approximately 7,000-8,000 voters who are receptive to the argument that government is not delivering results, that rents and living costs are rising faster than wages, or that the incumbent's policy agenda does not translate into concrete improvements to people's lives. In a district where 49.7% of voters rent and many of the remainder are young homebuyers or mortgage-burdened households, the opposition's core argument — that incumbent policies have failed to control costs or expand opportunity — has direct material resonance. Any candidate defending the incumbent seat must offer specific, credible answers on rent stabilization, tenant protections, wage floors, and the pathway from entry-level work to middle-class stability. The Asian American community (4.25%) and Hispanic community (2.78%), while smaller than in some comparison districts, are also present enough to be swing factors in a 4-point margin — and neither community's concerns around small business viability, healthcare access, and economic mobility can be taken for granted."
