          "The 84.65% white population, combined with the high income and education levels, suggests a district where racial diversity is limited but class diversity within the white constituency is significant — homeowners ranging from upper-middle-income professionals to affluent families with substantial financial assets. At 27.1% renters, there is a non-trivial renter minority (likely younger professionals or service-sector workers) whose economic concerns and housing costs may differ from the homeowner majority. The 8.53% Hispanic and 1.93% Black populations are small but present; in a district this affluent and white, explicit outreach to these communities by either party is relatively uncommon, which creates both a governance gap and a potential opening for opposition messaging that positions the incumbent as inattentive to minority constituent needs. The real durability test for the 71% margin is not whether the base holds, but whether the incumbent has built sufficient constituent goodwill and policy responsiveness among college-educated swing voters, renters, and minority communities that a credible Democratic challenger would need to run an unusually strong campaign to make the race competitive."
