          { name: "Property Tax Fairness and Cost-of-Living Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $79,376 median household income, 72.5% homeownership, and a mature 41.9-year median age, WI-SD-11 is a constituency with significant property equity stakes and fixed incomes for older adults. If property tax growth is outpacing income growth—a common condition in strong real estate markets—this creates direct economic pressure on the homeowner majority that can erode support even among naturally Republican voters. Addressing property tax fairness with specific policy remedies (assessment limits, exemption protection, or revenue diversification) is the most concrete way to hold this constituency's economic loyalty." },
          { name: "School Funding and Education Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29.9% college-attainment rate and mature median age indicate a constituency that values education and likely has children or grandchildren in local schools. In Wisconsin's funding landscape, where school quality is often tied to local property-tax-generated revenue, school funding adequacy is a material concern for this district's educated homeowner base. A candidate who can demonstrate that local schools are being resourced adequately and that state education policy is supporting—not undermining—school quality commands credibility with the primary stakeholder group in WI-SD-11." },
          { name: "Understanding the 42% Opposition Base", tag: "careful", why: "In a prosperous, educated, homeowner-dominant district, 42% Democratic opposition is not explained by economic desperation or demographic change alone—it likely reflects genuine ideological disagreement or specific governance concerns about how the district's considerable resources are being deployed. Whether the opposition is concentrated among renters (27.5%), younger adults, or residents of a particular geographic sub-area is a critical strategic fact that any candidate must understand. A challenger who can identify where this opposition is located and what specific grievance it reflects has a clearer path to growth than one who assumes it is simply the irreducible Democratic baseline." },
          { name: "Treating 58% as a Safe Majority Without Understanding What Holds It", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58-42 result in a district this prosperous, educated, and homeowner-dominant should be larger—and the fact that it is not suggests either that the incumbent is underperforming the district's natural Republican lean or that specific governance concerns are constraining what should be a stronger Republican margin. Treating 58% as a durable floor without investigating the gap between expected performance and actual performance is a strategic error that leaves the seat vulnerable to a challenger who understands the opposition's specific concerns." },
          { name: "Demographic Invisibility Around the 3.7% Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with a narrow 58-42 margin, the 3.7% demographic gap—most likely representing American Indian / Alaska Native, mixed-race, or other non-standard-category residents—cannot be strategically ignored. If this population contains voters who are mobilizable around tribal healthcare, education equity, or other specific concerns, or if they represent swing voters in a genuinely competitive district, treating them as invisible or negligible is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability. Any candidate should determine what this gap represents and whether it contains persuadable or mobilizable voters." }
