          { name: "Education Funding & Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29.5% college-attainment rate signals a constituency that prioritizes education—both for its own children and as a community asset. School funding adequacy, teacher compensation, and district capacity to invest in quality instruction are concrete quality-of-life issues for affluent families making multi-year residential decisions. In Wisconsin's competitive political environment, education funding has proven capable of mobilizing educated suburban voters across partisan lines; maintaining credibility on this issue is essential to retaining an educated base." },
          { name: "Measuring the Unopposed District's True Electoral Ceiling", tag: "careful", why: "A 100% unopposed result is not evidence of a 60-40 or 70-30 underlying base—it is evidence that no one tested the actual Democratic floor in this district. The 29.5% college-educated, $85,544-income, suburban/exurban profile could plausibly include 40-45% of voters who would support a credible Democratic challenger focused on education, healthcare, or tax fairness, and that ceiling remains unmeasured. The incumbent should treat this as an unknown variable rather than a permanent advantage and actively communicate to ensure retention if a challenger emerges." },
          { name: "Partisan Tribalism as a Strategic Anchor", tag: "avoid", why: "WI-SD-13's educated, affluent base is more likely to evaluate candidates on competence and local delivery than on partisan cultural messaging. A legislator who leads with culture-war positioning or tribal Republican signaling risks alienating the educated plurality who are evaluating on whether government is delivering for their families and communities. In Wisconsin's swing environment, affluent suburbanites have repeatedly abandoned candidates perceived as ideologically driven rather than outcomes-focused." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Victory is Durable Without Active Retention", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% unopposed result likely reflects the absence of Democratic recruitment and candidate quality in the last cycle, not the absence of electoral opportunity. A well-resourced Democratic challenger focused on education, healthcare costs, or property tax fairness could plausibly mobilize the renter minority (26.7%), younger professionals, and educated voters concerned about school funding. Treating unopposed as permanent mandate rather than temporary absence of competition is the most dangerous strategic error an incumbent can make in this district." }
