          { name: "Hispanic Working-Class Community Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 4.54% Hispanic population, while not numerically dominant, represents Wisconsin's fastest-growing demographic segment and is concentrated in working-class service and agricultural sectors where wages, workplace safety, and healthcare access are immediate economic concerns. In a 51-49 seat, a 2-3 percentage point shift in Hispanic voter turnout or candidate preference is mathematically sufficient to change the outcome. Intentional, specific engagement with Hispanic households on labor standards, apprenticeship access, and family healthcare—not generic minority outreach—is required to hold or win this seat." },
          { name: "Understanding the 49% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "A 51-49 result is not a mandate; it is a plurality of voters in a genuinely tied race where 49% of the electorate actively chose a different direction. Understanding whether that opposition is concentrated among working-class voters frustrated by economic stagnation, among non-college households skeptical of incumbent responsiveness, or among Hispanic voters underserved by current representation is strategically essential. In a seat this close, the opposition's driving concerns are not peripheral—they represent the swing constituency that determines control." },
          { name: "Treating 51% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51% result in a state Senate district is a plurality, not a mandate, and in a district where 49% voted for the other candidate and electoral momentum can shift rapidly, the incumbent should not assume continuity. The lack of demographic tailwinds (no youth bulge, no education-driven Democratic advantage, no urban concentration)—combined with a working-class electorate that evaluates on material economic outcomes rather than party loyalty—means this seat will remain competitive unless the incumbent actively demonstrates responsive governance." },
          { name: "Positioning Non-College Voters as a Backward or Mobilizable Block", tag: "avoid", why: "At only 20.6% college attainment, WI-SD-14 is not a college-educated, persuadable-from-the-left constituency—it is a working-class district where voters evaluate on material outcomes and have proven willing to vote split tickets based on incumbent performance. Any candidate who campaigns as though this constituency needs educating about policy, or who defaults to positioning non-college voters as a demographic to be mobilized rather than heard, will misread the district's actual electoral dynamics and lose persuadable households in the middle." }
