          "Engage the college-educated swing constituency on policy competence, not partisan loyalty: at 28.6% college attainment and upper-middle-class income, this district evaluates on whether the incumbent or challenger has actually delivered results or has a credible plan for doing so; rhetoric about party values or statewide priorities will not persuade voters in a 61-39 district where 39% already rejected the Democratic choice.",
          "Address the 28.6% renter population with specific housing and economic mobility policies: renters in an upper-middle-income district are likely young professionals or service-sector workers for whom affordable housing, student debt, childcare access, and pathways to homeownership are direct material concerns; this population is persuadable on grounds of whether government is actually solving their economic constraints.",
          "Recognize that 61% is a working majority, not a mandate: in a district where nearly two in five voters chose the Republican alternative, the incumbent or challenger must actively earn the persuadable college-educated center rather than assuming base loyalty; any campaign that treats 61% as a floor to build on rather than a fragile coalition to reinforce will face unexpected vulnerability."
