          { name: "Educated Voter Alignment on Governance Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 24.7% college attainment rate — above national average — signals a constituency that evaluates on demonstrated expertise and logical policy coherence rather than partisan tribal loyalty. Educated voters in their late 40s tend to have higher expectations for legislator competence: they read local budgets, they understand fiscal trade-offs, and they notice when a candidate's policy positions do not align with clearly stated priorities or local fiscal reality. Building credibility with this educated homeowner base requires demonstrating actual knowledge of district governance issues and the willingness to make defensible trade-offs rather than offering easy answers." },
          { name: "The ~6.5% Population Gap & Minority Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents several hundred district residents whose demographic and civic identity is not captured in the standard four categories. In Wisconsin, this gap may include American Indian communities, Alaska Native residents, or other populations with specific governance concerns around land use, water quality, or economic opportunity. This constituency should not be assumed to be safely Republican or safely Democratic; direct constituent service and engagement on issues that matter to them locally — rather than partisan assumption — builds both governing credibility and electoral durability." },
          { name: "Treating 57% as a Durable Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in Wisconsin, where recent statewide Republican performance has been 52-54%, is not a comfortable margin — it is a working plurality held by specific alignment between the incumbent's profile and older homeowner priorities. A candidate who campaigns as though 57% represents a secure base while neglecting the 43% opposition or assuming college-educated voters will remain reliably Republican regardless of governance performance is arithmetically misreading the district. Any challenger who can credibly demonstrate superior fiscal competence or better understanding of local governance will find this margin vulnerable." },
          { name: "Dismissing College-Educated Voters as Automatically Conservative", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated voters in their late 40s are not a monolithic voting bloc; they evaluate on specific policy positions, demonstrated competence, and whether government is delivering for them locally. A candidate whose campaign relies on the assumption that educated homeowners will support Republican candidates as a class — without demonstrating specific alignment on tax policy, school funding, or fiscal management — is ignoring the actual decision-making process of this constituency. College-educated swing voters in competitive districts have proven willing to support candidates of either party when they demonstrate competence and alignment on material governance concerns." }
