        issues: [
          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Pressures", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $71,175 median income with 71% homeownership and a 43-year median age, WI-SD-29 is a middle-class homeowner community for whom property taxes, insurance costs, utilities, and healthcare expenses are material budget constraints. The Republican margin of 62% reflects alignment with this demographic, but that alignment is contingent on government delivering tangible protection of homeowner economic security. A legislative agenda focused on property tax transparency, homeowner insurance cost management, and utility rate oversight directly addresses the material concerns of the 71% homeowner majority and is the most durable foundation for holding the seat against Democratic economic messaging." },
          { name: "Renter & Younger Adult Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 26.4% renter rate represents a meaningful constituency—roughly one-quarter of the district—whose economic concerns center on wages, housing costs, and mobility toward homeownership rather than property tax management. The 43-year median age signals aging in place, but also a cohort of younger adults in the district evaluating economic opportunity. Housing affordability, wage growth, and the pathway from renting to first-time homeownership are the concrete concerns for this group, and a Republican incumbent who addresses these directly—rather than assuming renter voters are lost to Democrats—can consolidate the younger portion of the coalition." },
          { name: "Democratic Baseline Mobilization in Exurban & Suburban Districts", tag: "careful", why: "The 38% Democratic result in WI-SD-29 is consistent with Democratic performance gains in middle-class suburban and exurban districts across Wisconsin and the Upper Midwest in recent cycles. This is not a hidden or dormant opposition—it is an active baseline with organizational capacity and messaging discipline. A 62-38 margin is durable for an incumbent who actively manages homeowner economic concerns and delivers on constituent service, but it is not insulated from a credible Democratic challenger who speaks to cost-of-living pressures with specificity and local knowledge. Treating 38% as simply 'the other party's voters' rather than as a potential swing constituency is the most common Republican error in districts like this one." },
          { name: "Idealizing Property Rights or Tax Philosophy Over Homeowner Fiscal Reality", tag: "avoid", why: "A homeowner-majority, middle-income constituency does not vote based on abstract principles about property rights or tax philosophy—it votes based on whether government is protecting household budgets and delivering core services without imposing surprise cost increases. Any candidate whose messaging emphasizes ideological tax reduction or property-rights positioning while remaining silent on actual property tax rates, insurance costs, or school funding crises is misreading the constituency. Homeowners in WI-SD-29 want government to work effectively and affordably; they are not voting to validate a political philosophy." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as a Safe Seat or Permission to Neglect Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 result in a state where Democratic performance in middle-class districts has been trending upward is a solid margin but not a landslide, and it is certainly not permission to assume the seat is safely held. Any incumbent who consolidates only the existing Republican base, avoids town halls or direct constituent engagement on cost-of-living concerns, or assumes that party affiliation alone will deliver reelection in the next cycle is creating the conditions for a competitive race. This seat requires active, ongoing constituent attention—particularly to the renter and younger adult portions of the district that are not inherited Republican voters." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "WI-SD-29: A Solidly Republican, Aging Homeowner District at $71K Income—Durable but Not Dynamic, Requiring Active Engagement on Cost-of-Living Pressures to Hold Against Democratic Mobilization.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "WI-SD-29 is a demographically stable, economically settled district where Tomczyk's 62-38 victory reflects a genuine and meaningful Republican alignment—not a precarious plurality or a margin threatened by hidden opposition. The district's profile is straightforward: 87.18% white, 71.2% homeownership (inverse of the 26.4% renter rate), $71,175 median household income, 43.0 median age, and 25.6% college attainment. This is a middle-income, predominantly homeowner community anchored by working and middle-class families who have accumulated modest property assets and whose economic security depends on stable housing costs, property tax management, and predictable local service delivery. The 62% result is consistent with this demographic foundation—Republican alignment among homeowner-majority, middle-income constituencies is durable across most of the Upper Midwest. However, the district's strength is also its constraint: with only 25.6% college attainment and a $71K income that is solid but not affluent, this constituency is genuinely vulnerable to economic disruption, and any candidate who takes that 62% as permission to ignore cost-of-living pressures will find the margin narrower in the next cycle.",
          "The strategic vulnerability in WI-SD-29 is not demographic—it is economic. At $71,175 income with a 43-year median age, this is a constituency for whom property taxes, homeowner insurance costs, utility rates, and healthcare expenses are material line items in household budgets. The 26.4% renter rate, while modest, also represents a constituency with less asset cushion and greater sensitivity to wage stagnation and housing cost pressures. The 38% Democratic baseline is not insignificant in a state where Democratic performance in suburban and exurban districts has been trending upward; the question for any Republican incumbent or challenger is whether the existing 62-38 margin reflects genuine constituent satisfaction with how government is managing cost-of-living pressures, or whether it reflects simple partisan inheritance that could erode if Democrats field a candidate who speaks credibly to homeowner economic security. The 5.2% demographic gap (white population plus reported minorities summing to 92.46%) is small enough to be demographic noise, so the strategic analysis is not about hidden constituencies—it is about whether the Republican incumbent can demonstrate that he is actively managing the material concerns of the homeowner majority whose economic security underpins the seat."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with homeowner economic security as an affirmative agenda: property tax fairness and predictability, homeowner insurance cost management, and utility rate transparency are the material governance concerns for a $71K-income, 71%-homeowner constituency and the most direct way to defend the 62% margin against Democratic economic messaging.",
          "Engage renters and younger adults specifically on housing affordability and wage growth; the 26.4% renter rate and 43-year median age together represent a constituency that is aging in place and gradually upgrading from rental to ownership, making first-time homebuyer affordability and local wage trends the swing factors for the younger portion of the district.",
          "Avoid abstract ideological positioning on property rights or tax philosophy; this is a constituency that owns property and wants it protected, but also wants government to deliver on its core functions—schools, infrastructure, emergency services—without imposing surprise cost increases that erode household budgets.",
          "Recognize that 38% opposition is a real baseline, not a rounding error; in a state where Democratic performance has improved in middle-class suburban and exurban districts, any incumbent who treats 62% as a safe seat rather than a coalition requiring active management on cost-of-living concerns will create opening for a credible challenger."
        ]
